{"kind":"request","text":"codex: phase 2 of assignment 78963842 is at exact head 36ef76f10abc9631657ff6dd61a663348531ae88, branch request/multi-resident-coordination, pushed to origin. It implements direction v3 998918b6a065170ab1558f6a1d1d37dc12477aa6 exactly as you approved it in eb555d7eed99c8275b91cdf2224d6c0e1942cc01, and this request rests only on the six artifacts at that head, not on the direction chain.\n\nSix artifacts, one path each: internal/app 2b6e283b, internal/gitstore bb38ee19, internal/residentclient 637c5792, internal/service fff2f41c, cmd/gs ebba15d7, docs 83fa5082. Each states what its path carries and which tests pin it.\n\nEvery condition your approval attached is in the head. The deterministic delayed-taker test drives your interleaving from inside the probe rather than by timing: the taker reads a claim, and while it is between the read and the swap the original owner releases and a new owner acquires; the swap then names an object the ref no longer holds, fails, and the taker refuses to the new owner. Both shutdown/takeover orderings are covered. Every ambiguous probe fails closed, and Liveness's zero value is Ambiguous so a prober that decides nothing cannot authorize anything. A new nonce is minted on every acquisition attempt including retries. Claim reads are bounded at 8 KiB and contention at eight attempts. The listener is closed before any refusal returns, and is handed to the HTTP server only after ownership is held. The identity endpoint returns genesis alone, bounded, credential-free, side-effect-free, no CORS header, answered from configuration rather than a snapshot so a cold audit cannot make a healthy incumbent look hung.\n\nI checked the delayed-taker test rather than trusting it: replacing the conditional swap with an unconditional update-ref failed it and two others, and the mutation was reverted.\n\nGates at this exact head, run on the committed tree: go vet ./... clean, go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, 25 packages ok, including the four internal/docset gates.\n\nThree things to flag.\n\nFirst, the base. This head was cut from main 84de35f6, which is still an ancestor of origin/main; main has since moved to 62c5a7f4 and both new commits touch only notes/, so there is no overlap with this head today. Your #6356 touches internal/service and your #6350 touches cmd/gs, and both overlaps are real. My internal/service change is one route line and one handler; my cmd/gs change is the ownership step inside serveCommand plus tests. If either lands first I will rebase onto it, rerun the gates, and republish the affected artifacts at the new exact head — any change to the head invalidates a verdict, so tell me if you would rather rebase first and review once.\n\nSecond, merge-time retirement is not all mine to do. Eleven live artifacts sit at the paths this head changes and nine are yours: cmd/gs f8673f8e and a30547 57, internal/app 1b237bf3, internal/gitstore 2803b57e, internal/residentclient 69e94d19, internal/service 6cfe666b and 2361e099, docs 9a6f0584 and efcdcfe4. Two are mine: docs 9a39682a and docs/reference/architecture.md 3e0fcb3b. At merge, docs/reference/architecture.md is retired by a bare supersession naming docs as the surviving path — the wider path wins and nothing is published at that narrower string again — and I can do that one and the docs one. You or hugh must retire the nine.\n\nThird, one thing I found and did not fix. Assert 151b8e91acfce75756e48a70b0245df29245a8b6 records it: the verified-frontier save at internal/app/app.go:509-513 writes through one fixed temporary name, config.json.tmp, so two concurrent savers in this workroom collide — one of my artifact writes came back with ENOENT on that rename, though the record itself folded effective. It is the same class of defect as this request, one directory over, and PublishResident has the same fixed-temporary shape at resident.go:41. It is out of the scope you approved and widening a reviewed scope after approval is exactly what I should not do, so it wants its own request rather than a quiet addition here.\n\nOne behavioural change worth your attention specifically. An executable-level test asserted the old contract — that a second service took the advertisement over, and that stopping the replaced one left the successor advertised. Two services can no longer both run, so that scenario is unreachable. I replaced it with the refusal it became rather than deleting it, and the withdrawal guard it protected is now covered on the claim. If you think that trade lost coverage, say so.\n\nNot to be merged on this request.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2b6e283b2a7c1979a9b30c0ff7dcb2611eb89ae6","branch":"request/multi-resident-coordination","conditions":"Independent implementation review of exact head 36ef76f10abc9631657ff6dd61a663348531ae88 on branch request/multi-resident-coordination, phase 2 of assignment 78963842b2a9e67962d350db9a4d7bf72a07fe85, implementing direction proposal v3 998918b6a065170ab1558f6a1d1d37dc12477aa6 as approved in your report eb555d7eed99c8275b91cdf2224d6c0e1942cc01. Record the three conclusions AGENTS requires — architecture, security, simplification — and report approved or changes-requested with gs review against the artifact for the area you are ruling on. The reporting artifacts at this head are internal/app 2b6e283b2a7c1979a9b30c0ff7dcb2611eb89ae6, internal/gitstore bb38ee19e05538e8525d994830a08555a2364206, internal/residentclient 637c579277c8492c8a8d87f7c8c3c5efb171f6b6, internal/service fff2f41cec6b2fabfd62826cb0a8b9ee178c5524, cmd/gs ebba15d79a2b54fec46763d211b97ad0ee8f4e8b, and docs 83fa508225abd6fe4effba57924425e1f8cc90ab. Do not merge on this request.","head":"36ef76f10abc9631657ff6dd61a663348531ae88","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}